// LIFETIMES ARE NOT INFERRED, AND NOT CHECKED. Behavioural half of a pair with
// tests/reject/uncalled_body_is_checked.pd.
//
// `dangling` returns a reference to a local that dies at the closing brace. It
// is the canonical lifetime error -- the first thing any lifetime analysis
// rejects -- and this file compiles, links and runs.
//
// The row this fixture serves used to argue the point from two identifier
// spellings: `grep -rn 'region\|Region' src/` is empty, therefore nothing could
// infer a lifetime. That does not follow, and it was written in the same row
// that had just recorded 83 real readers of lifetime_params. Absence of a name
// is not absence of a behaviour. This is the behaviour.
//
// WHY main() DOES NOT CALL IT
// Calling it dereferences a dead stack slot. Measured, it printed 8261746944
// and exited 0; recording that in a transcript would bless undefined behaviour
// as expected output, which is the failure tests/stdlib/ exists to prevent.
// What is asserted here is that THE FILE COMPILES -- that no phase refused it.
// The companion reject fixture proves the refusal machinery does run over
// uncalled bodies, so "it compiles" is a statement about lifetimes and not
// about dead-code elimination.
//
// PROMOTION: when lifetimes are checked, this file stops compiling, conformance
// reports COMPILE_FAIL, and the row moves to tests/reject/ with the diagnostic
// pinned. Do not "fix" this file; the gap is in the compiler.
fn dangling() -> &i64 {
    let x: i64 = 5;
    return &x;
}

fn main() {
    print("lifetimes_uninferred_fixture");
    print("dangling() is defined above and deliberately not called");
}
